        "id": "ch1_6_2-2",
        "text": "will manage all the DNS A records (an A record is a mapping between IPv4\naddresses and domain names):\nroot@kali:~# host ds-server.ksec.local\nds-server.ksec.local has address 10.0.0.177\nIf you specify a nonexisting DNS record, you will get an error message (this\nis useful to brute-force the DNS records):\nroot@kali:~# host hello.ksec.local\nHost hello.ksec.local not found: 3(NXDOMAIN)\n46\nChapter 1 I Mastering the Terminal Window\nTake note that you can add your own static DNS records inside your Kali\nhost. The file is located at /etc/hosts , and here you can redirect any domain\nname to any live IP address. (This is how DNS poisoning works; the hacker will\nmanipulate the A records to point to his server IP address.)\nroot@kali:~# cat /etc/hosts\n127.0.0.1       localhost\n127.0.1.1       kali\n# The following lines are desirable for IPv6 capable hosts\n::1     localhost ip6-localhost ip6-loopback\nff02::1 ip6-allnodes\nff02::2 ip6-allrouters\nYou\u2019ll learn more about this subject later in this book, and you will learn how\nDNS brute-forcing and zone transfers work.",
